From: Matthew Kelch <kelchm@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: CPPC: set the CPPC Enable register at policy init

The DGX Spark (GB10, BIOS 5.36) firmware ignores performance requests
until OSPM sets the ACPI CPPC Enable register. cppc_cpufreq never sets
it, so on a mainline-based kernel every core stays at its lowest
performance level. Call cppc_set_enable() when the policy is
initialized, and tolerate platforms without the register (-EOPNOTSUPP).

This mirrors what NVIDIA's linux-nvidia kernel does. An equivalent
change is posted for mainline (Sumit Gupta's CPPC Enable series, v2,
2026-11-10) but not merged. Drop this patch once that series lands in
the pinned Linux version.

Measured on two DGX Sparks (BIOS 5.36): 20-thread STREAM copy 78 ->
129 GB/s, single-thread 37 -> 55 GB/s, inference engine start 179 s ->
93 s.

Applies with --fuzz=0 to the file as shipped in Linux 6.18.58.
---
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cppc_cpufreq.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cppc_cpufreq.c
@@ -598,6 +598,15 @@
 	policy->driver_data = cpu_data;
 
 	/*
+	 * Enable CPPC for both OS-driven and autonomous modes, as NVIDIA's
+	 * linux-nvidia kernel does. GB10 firmware ignores performance requests
+	 * until OSPM sets CPPC Enable; the register is optional elsewhere.
+	 */
+	ret = cppc_set_enable(cpu, true);
+	if (ret && ret != -EOPNOTSUPP)
+		pr_warn("Failed to enable CPPC for CPU%d (%d)\n", cpu, ret);
+
+	/*
 	 * Set min to lowest nonlinear perf to avoid any efficiency penalty (see
 	 * Section 8.4.7.1.1.5 of ACPI 6.1 spec)
 	 */
